Linux Software Uninstaller for Debian
=====================================

Linux Software Uninstaller is a graphical tool that removes applications
together with their configuration and leftover files — an IObit
Uninstaller–style program for Debian and KDE Plasma. It handles APT (.deb)
packages, Flatpak, Snap, AppImage and programs unpacked by hand into /opt.

The package targets Debian 13 "trixie" (Python 3.13, PyQt6, KDE Plasma 6).
It should also work on other desktops as long as a polkit agent is running.


Starting
--------

From the menu: System → Linux Software Uninstaller
From a terminal: linux-software-uninstaller

Run it as a normal user. Do not use "sudo linux-software-uninstaller" — the
program asks for the administrator password itself when it needs it.


Language
--------

The interface is available in English (default) and Polish. Change it in
Settings → Language; "Automatic" follows the system language ($LANG).
The change takes effect after a restart.


How privileges work
-------------------

The window never runs as root. Operations that need administrator rights
are performed by a separate helper:

  /usr/libexec/linux-software-uninstaller/lsu-helper

started through pkexec using the polkit action:

  linux-software-uninstaller.helper
  (/usr/share/polkit-1/actions/linux-software-uninstaller.policy)

By default the administrator password is required (auth_admin_keep) and
polkit remembers it for a few minutes. The helper accepts only the commands
"uninstall", "remove-paths" and "cleanup", validates package names, and
refuses paths outside the allowed folders as well as files owned by
installed packages.

An administrator can change the rules with a polkit rule, e.g. in
/etc/polkit-1/rules.d/50-linux-software-uninstaller.rules:

  polkit.addRule(function(action, subject) {
      if (action.id == "linux-software-uninstaller.helper" && subject.isInGroup("sudo")) {
          return polkit.Result.AUTH_ADMIN_KEEP;
      }
  });


Backup before uninstalling
--------------------------

Enabled in Settings (on by default). The method is chosen automatically:

  * btrfs with snapper configured — snapper snapshot ("root" config),
    restore with: snapper rollback
  * btrfs without snapper — read-only snapshot in
    /.snapshots/lsu-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS
  * another file system (e.g. ext4) with Timeshift — Timeshift restore
    point; Timeshift has to be configured once beforehand
  * anything else — configuration backup in
    /var/backups/linux-software-uninstaller/<date>/:
        packages.txt     removed packages with their versions
        config.tar.gz    their configuration files (conffiles) from /etc
        other.txt        removed Flatpak, Snap and /opt programs
        restore.sh       restore script

    To restore:
        sudo sh /var/backups/linux-software-uninstaller/<date>/restore.sh

    The folder is readable by root only (it may contain passwords from
    /etc). The 20 newest backups are kept. This backup does not include
    Flatpak data or the contents of /opt folders.

If a full snapshot fails, the program still saves the configuration backup
before uninstalling anything.


Leftover files
--------------

After uninstalling, the program looks for files left behind:

  * in your home folder: ~/.config, ~/.local/share, ~/.local/state,
    ~/.cache, ~/.var/app (Flatpak), autostart, menu entries, user systemd
    units, ~/.local/bin and hidden files in the home folder,
  * system-wide: /etc, /var/lib, /var/cache, /var/log, /opt, /usr/local,
    APT repositories and their keys (sources.list.d, keyrings,
    trusted.gpg.d), systemd units and autostart entries.

"Confirmed" matches are pre-selected, "possible" ones must be selected by
hand. Files owned by installed packages and by programs that are still
installed are skipped. Files from the home folder go to the Trash by
default (restore them in Dolphin); system files are deleted permanently.


Files
-----

  /usr/bin/linux-software-uninstaller                     launcher
  /usr/share/linux-software-uninstaller/                  Python modules
  /usr/libexec/linux-software-uninstaller/                root helper
  /usr/share/polkit-1/actions/                            polkit action
  ~/.config/linux-software-uninstaller/                   user settings
  ~/.local/state/linux-software-uninstaller/activity.log  activity log
  /var/backups/linux-software-uninstaller/                configuration backups


Upgrading from "deinstalator"
-----------------------------

This package replaces the old "deinstalator" package; apt removes the old
one automatically. On first start, settings and the activity log are copied
from the old locations (~/.config/deinstalator,
~/.local/state/deinstalator). Old backups stay in /var/backups/deinstalator.

If you used version 1.0 (a single script), remove it by hand:

  rm ~/.local/bin/deinstalator ~/.local/share/applications/deinstalator.desktop


Removing Linux Software Uninstaller
-----------------------------------

  sudo apt purge linux-software-uninstaller

Settings and the log in your home folder, and backups in
/var/backups/linux-software-uninstaller, are kept — remove them by hand if
you do not need them:

  rm -r ~/.config/linux-software-uninstaller ~/.local/state/linux-software-uninstaller
  sudo rm -r /var/backups/linux-software-uninstaller


Troubleshooting
---------------

No password prompt appears
  No polkit agent is running. On KDE Plasma it is provided by
  polkit-kde-agent-1. Check with: dpkg -l polkit-kde-agent-1

"apt does not allow removing these packages"
  Another APT operation is running (e.g. Discover or automatic updates) or
  the system has unmet dependencies. Try: sudo apt -f install

Missing program icon in the menu or window
  Install SVG support for Qt 6: sudo apt install qt6-svg-plugins

Timeshift does not work
  Run "sudo timeshift-gtk" or "sudo timeshift --setup" once and choose a
  backup device.

Removing a snap or flatpak fails
  Make sure the program is not running and try again.

Interface errors are printed when the program is started from a terminal:
  linux-software-uninstaller


Building the package from source
--------------------------------

  sudo apt install python3 dpkg-dev
  ./packaging/build-deb.sh
  sudo apt install ./linux-software-uninstaller_*_all.deb

